<Guide
  title="BPPV Keeps Coming Back? Why It Recurs and How to Be Ready"
  description="Why BPPV keeps coming back after the Epley maneuver, how often it recurs, what's linked to it, and how to be ready so the next episode is shorter."
  path="/guides/bppv-keeps-coming-back/"
  faq={faq}
  sources={sources}
>
  <p class="answer">
    BPPV keeps coming back because a treatment moves the loose crystals out of the canal but doesn't
    stop more crystals from coming loose later. Recurrence is common: in one year-long follow-up after
    successful treatment, 26% of patients had it return,<cite><a href="#src-1">[1]</a></cite> and
    studies that follow people longer report higher rates. It's usually the same condition returning,
    not a new or worse one — but a recurrence that feels different deserves a fresh check.
  </p>

  <h2>What's actually coming back</h2>
  <p>
    In BPPV, small calcium carbonate crystals (otoconia) that normally sit in one part of the inner
    ear come loose and drift into one of the semicircular canals. Moving your head then moves them,
    and your brain reads that as spinning. The Epley maneuver moves them back out of the posterior
    canal. It doesn't reattach them or stop others from breaking loose, so another episode is
    possible whenever more crystals drift into a canal <mark>[VERIFY]</mark>.
  </p>

  <h2>How often it recurs</h2>
  <p>
    Numbers vary a lot between studies, partly because of how long patients are followed.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      In a study that followed patients for one year after their BPPV cleared with treatment, it did
      not recur in 70%, recurred in 26%, and persisted in 4%.<cite><a href="#src-1">[1]</a></cite>
    </li>
    <li>
      The same authors note that published recurrence rates range from about 4% to 50%, and that the
      longer patients are followed, the higher the proportion that recur.<cite><a href="#src-1">[1]</a></cite>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    So if yours has come back, you're in a large group, not an unusual one.
  </p>

  <h2>What's linked to it coming back</h2>
  <p>
    Research has looked at many possible risk factors. The one with the most direct evidence for doing
    something about it is vitamin D.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Low vitamin D.</strong> A 2024 review found that in BPPV patients with vitamin D
      deficiency or insufficiency (below 30 ng/mL), supplementation reduced both the number of people
      who relapsed and the number of relapses per person.<cite><a href="#src-2">[2]</a></cite> In one
      of the trials it covered, recurrence fell from 46.7% to 37.8%.<cite><a href="#src-2">[2]</a></cite>
      This applies to people who are deficient — it isn't evidence that extra vitamin D helps people
      with normal levels.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Other factors studied</strong> include older age, female sex, head injury, and
      conditions such as osteoporosis, diabetes and high blood pressure <mark>[VERIFY]</mark>. These
      are associations from observational studies, and most aren't things you change for the sake of
      your BPPV.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Being ready for the next episode</h2>
  <p>
    Since you can't reliably prevent it, the useful thing is making the next episode shorter. Most of
    that is done between episodes, while you're well:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Know your side and your maneuver.</strong> Write down which ear and which maneuver your
      clinician identified last time. Mid-episode is the worst time to work it out.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Know what "same as last time" means.</strong> Note what triggered it (lying back, rolling
      left) and how long each spin lasted. If the next episode matches, you and your clinician have a
      reference point. If it doesn't — other side, different trigger, spinning that lasts minutes
      rather than seconds, or any new symptoms — get it checked rather than repeating the old plan.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ask about vitamin D.</strong> If it hasn't been tested, ask whether it should be.<cite><a href="#src-2">[2]</a></cite>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ask whether to rehearse the positions.</strong> Getting the angles right is much easier
      to learn when turning your head doesn't make the room spin — but whether rehearsing between
      episodes is appropriate for you is your clinician's call <mark>[VERIFY]</mark>. See
      <a href="/guides/epley-maneuver-angles/">Epley maneuver angles</a>.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Examples</h2>
  <h3>Same side, same trigger</h3>
  <p>
    Eight months after a right-ear episode that cleared with the Epley maneuver, rolling onto the
    right side in bed brings back the same short spin. It matches their notes from last time. Their
    clinician had shown them the maneuver for that side, so they know what to do, and they know that
    if it isn't better after a few rounds, the next step is an appointment.
  </p>
  <h3>A recurrence that isn't the same</h3>
  <p>
    Someone who's had left-sided episodes before now gets spinning when rolling either way, lasting
    longer than it used to. Doing the usual left-ear maneuver would be treating the last episode, not
    this one. A different pattern is a reason to be re-examined — it may be a different canal, the
    other side, or something other than BPPV.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p style="margin-top:0">
      BPPVCoach is built for the time between episodes: if your clinician agrees, rehearse the
      positions with live angle feedback while you're well, and keep a record on your device of each session and how your
      symptoms changed, so the next episode starts with more to go on than memory.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/session/">Start a guided session</a> · <a href="/how-it-works/">How it works</a></p>
  </div>
</Guide>
